{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nOn the Gerrit `Verified` gate, `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` intermittently crashes its pytest-xdist\nworker (`worker gwN crashed`; `[gwN] node down: Not properly terminated`), failing the whole matrix job and\nproducing a spurious `Verified -1`. The failure is a hard worker-process death (not an assertion), lands on a\nRANDOM matrix shard per run (~1 of 4), and is timing/platform-dependent. Observed 4+ consecutive times on\n2026-08-08 blocking change 1545 (ticket ad16), whose diff is markdown/comment-only and cannot touch\n`opcert_service`. Reproduction is CI-only (never locally), so the fix is chosen for defensibility under xdist\nload, not for a local repro.\n\n## Root cause\nThe background worker runs each job with `asyncio.to_thread(jobs.run_job, ...)` (`src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py`\nline ~77) -- i.e. on the event loop's **shared default `ThreadPoolExecutor`**. The lifespan teardown\n(`app.py` ~55-59) cancels and awaits the *asyncio task*, but `asyncio.to_thread` gives NO handle to the\nunderlying OS thread and cancelling the awaiting task does not stop that thread. So the observation that the\nlifespan \"cancels and awaits its worker tasks correctly\" is true only of the asyncio task -- the\ndefault-executor worker THREAD is never joined by teardown. Under xdist load, when the `TestClient` context\nexits and tears down the ASGI lifespan + anyio portal (and the event loop), an executor thread from `to_thread`\ncan still be alive and referencing the closing loop, producing a hard interpreter/worker death. That thread\norphaning is the concrete defect; the previously-proposed \"make the `with TestClient(...) as c:` exit\nguaranteed\" change is a no-op because the fixture already uses a `with` block.\n\n## Plan\nPrimary fix (root cause, `src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py`): give the app its OWN `ThreadPoolExecutor` created\nin `lifespan` startup, run `jobs.run_job` on it via `loop.run_in_executor(app.state.executor, ...)` instead of\n`asyncio.to_thread` (which is hard-wired to the shared default executor), and in the teardown `finally` block --\nafter cancelling/awaiting the worker tasks -- call `app.state.executor.shutdown(wait=True, cancel_futures=True)`\nso every worker thread is deterministically joined BEFORE the lifespan (and the loop/portal) finishes. No\norphan thread can then outlive the event loop at `TestClient` teardown. Request/response behavior and the\nper-run timeout semantics are unchanged (`asyncio.wait_for` still wraps the awaitable).\n\nDefense-in-depth (blast-radius containment, `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py`): pin this module to a\nSINGLE xdist worker with `pytest.mark.xdist_group(\"opcert_service_app\")` in `pytestmark`, following the existing\nprecedent at `tests/integration/test_reconcile_live_e2e.py:65`. Rationale: even if a residual teardown race\nsurvives the executor fix, co-locating this module's tests removes the cross-shard interaction so a teardown\nrace can no longer fell a RANDOM unrelated matrix shard -- the named blast-radius problem -- turning a\nwhole-matrix `Verified -1` into, at worst, a contained local failure. This uses only the already-declared\n`pytest-xdist==3.8.0` (pyproject.toml:217); no new test tooling is introduced.\n\nScope is the two files in file_impact; no public API or request/response contract changes.\n\n## Testing\nAll commands use only already-declared tooling (pytest + pytest-xdist); no `pytest-repeat`/`--count`.\n- Repeated xdist load must show no worker death:\n  `for i in $(seq 1 20); do .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -n 4 -q 2>&1; done | grep -c \"node down\"`\n  returns `0`.\n- Single-process run still passes: `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -q`.\n- The module is confirmed pinned to one worker: `grep -n 'xdist_group(\"opcert_service_app\")' tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py`\n  returns a match.\n- The app no longer uses the shared default executor for jobs:\n  `grep -n \"asyncio.to_thread\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns `0` matches and\n  `grep -n \"run_in_executor\\|ThreadPoolExecutor\\|executor.shutdown\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` shows the\n  app-owned executor + its teardown shutdown.\n- Durable CI confirmation: the `Verified` matrix goes green on this change and stays green across at least 3\n  consecutive `recheck` re-runs with no `worker gwN crashed` / `node down` line in any job log.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` runs `jobs.run_job` on an app-owned `ThreadPoolExecutor` (created in\n      `lifespan` startup) via `run_in_executor`, NOT `asyncio.to_thread`\n      (`grep -n \"asyncio.to_thread\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns 0 matches).\n- [ ] The `lifespan` teardown joins every worker thread before completing by calling\n      `app.state.executor.shutdown(wait=True, cancel_futures=True)` after the task cancel/await\n      (`grep -n \"executor.shutdown\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns a match).\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` pins the module to a single xdist worker via\n      `pytest.mark.xdist_group(\"opcert_service_app\")`\n      (`grep -n 'xdist_group(\"opcert_service_app\")' tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` returns a match).\n- [ ] Under repeated xdist load the worker no longer dies:\n      `for i in $(seq 1 20); do .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -n 4 -q 2>&1; done | grep -c \"node down\"`\n      returns `0`, and `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -q` passes.\n- [ ] The `Verified` CI matrix is green on this change and stays green across at least 3 consecutive re-runs with\n      no `worker gwN crashed` / `node down` line in any job log."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786238830545323001,"uuid":"3c58e9ce-23a7-40c0-a0db-34b066c27686"}